  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The two paths in this view",
      "items": [
        "Advisory (solid): POST /api/v1/policy/check resolves context, runs the evaluator and returns findings. It creates no claim, writes no finding, records no audit event and changes no status (policy_check_service.py:1-6).",
        "Persisted (solid, human only): a reviewer decision writes the status change and its audit event in one transaction (expense_workflow_service.py:177-207).",
        "Dashed: the engine's findings are never written. evaluate_expense() has exactly one caller - the advisory pre-flight - and the steps 8-9 persistence module it names, app/services/policy_service.py, does not exist in this revision (evaluator.py:8-11). No Finding row is ever constructed outside app/models/review.py."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Tri-state result: PASS / FAIL / UNKNOWN",
      "items": [
        "PASS: reliable information exists and the rule is satisfied.",
        "FAIL: reliable information exists and the rule is breached. A FAIL must carry the expected condition or the calculation that produced it, in code and in the database (policy/base.py:169-175, models/review.py:124-127).",
        "UNKNOWN: required fields, context or reliable evidence are missing. It is a legitimate, first-class outcome and is never converted into PASS - it routes to an employee correction, a reviewer assessment or a notice, and that routing is carried on the finding (policy/base.py:8-16).",
        "With no approved policy provisioned the registry holds no rule, so every claim is reported UNKNOWN rather than looking clean (evaluator.py:218-237, registry.py:77-79)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "The real audit guarantee - nothing more",
      "items": [
        "audit_events is append-only because the database refuses: trigger trg_audit_events_append_only (BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ... RAISE EXCEPTION, ERRCODE restrict_violation) is installed by migration 0001 (migration 0001:1850-1857).",
        "That is a database-level refusal of UPDATE and DELETE. It is NOT cryptographic immutability, NOT WORM storage, NOT tamper-evidence (nobody hashes or chains the rows) and NOT a claim of legal admissibility (models/audit.py:69-72).",
        "Atomicity comes from the request transaction, not the trigger: one session per request commits when the handler returns and rolls back on any exception, so a decision and its audit event either both persist or neither does (api/deps.py:60-98, db/session.py:75-91)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "What this engine does not do",
      "items": [
        "It never auto-approves, auto-rejects or pays anything. A finding is a flag; a finance reviewer decides (expense_workflow_service.py:177-207). No payment or reimbursement execution exists anywhere in this revision.",
        "Duplicate candidates are signals, not fraud determinations - and app/duplicates/signals.py has no caller at all, so candidate generation is not wired (evaluator.py:412-414).",
        "No currency conversion: no approved rate source exists, so step 6 is skipped and that fact is recorded instead of an invented rate (evaluator.py:356-364).",
        "Finding persistence and resolution are declared unimplemented by the API's own capability report (meta_service.py:30-46), and no endpoint resolves or dismisses a finding: schemas/review.py models it, nothing routes it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Evidence, rule parameters and idempotency",
      "items": [
        "Every finding carries message, employee_message, evidence (JSONB), calculation, expected_value, observed_value, recommended_action and a source_reference back to the policy clause (models/review.py:86-100).",
        "Thresholds are not in code: a rule can only be active with a verified source clause and a named human validator (source_requires_validation, models/policy.py:145-154). The rule implementation is looked up by evaluator_type string, never stored as a callable.",
        "The evaluation fingerprint hashes the claim version, the policy version and the resolved scope, so re-running evaluation for identical inputs cannot produce a second finding (context/scope.py:69-107, models/review.py:98-100).",
        "Fields are read from provenance rows, not the denormalised claim columns, so employee-entered text is never treated as receipt evidence (context/scope.py:129-135)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "The demo policy is fiction, and the clients are not wired",
      "items": [
        "docs/demo-company-expense-policy.md opens with 'This policy is fiction'; it is not an employer policy and not legal, tax or accounting guidance. Its clause ids (DEMO-EXP-NNN) live in policy_rules.source_clause and the version is labelled DEMO ONLY / FICTIONAL (docs:1-9, docs:116-121).",
        "It seeds only when APP_ENV is development or test AND DEMO_MODE is true AND DEMO_SYNTHETIC_POLICY_ENABLED is true, a flag that defaults to false; with it off the registry loads no fictional rule and claims evaluate to UNKNOWN rather than to a fictional PASS (registry.py:343-348, demo-company-expense-policy.md:83-94).",
        "The pre-flight is registered and generated into the shared client types (packages/api-client/src/schema.d.ts:373-386), but no shipped screen calls it or the decision endpoint: apps/portal and apps/console only configure an /api/v1 base URL, and the console's audit surface labels itself 'Scaffold · no audit workflow implemented' (apps/console/src/lib/api.ts:29-34, AppShell.tsx:99-101)."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
